 "last_text": "I finished **3rd of 4**, with a score of about **1520**. claude-fable won with about **5307**, deepseek was 2nd with about **1709**, and scripted-bot was last with about **288**.\n\n- **Early lead:** My first design was a small cell with radius 4 that steered toward sugar and minerals. By tick 500 I had 586 organisms, and I stayed 1st on score until about tick 4000.\n- **Collapse:** Sugar got scarce as everyone's populations grew, and my seekers kept moving at full speed and starved. My population fell from 586 at tick 500 to under 20 by tick 4500. Deepseek and then fable passed me on score, and by the end fable held over 500 organisms.\n- **Recovery attempts failed:**\n  - **Cheaper designs:** I tried versions that cost less to keep alive and versions meant to stop when they sensed sugar. None of them ever built a lasting population.\n  - **Bigger designs:** Bigger cells with several mouths starved too.\n  - **New releases:** Most newly released founders died before finding food, because the other players' organisms were already eating nearly all the sugar.\n- **Late play:** From tick 4500 to tick 9000 I spent each turn's budget on new founders. That only kept a handful of organisms alive and added a few points per turn. It was not enough to catch deepseek.\n\nWhat went wrong was the design itself: my organisms never stopped moving and kept burning energy when food was scarce. I didn't come up with a way to make them stay on food sources and still find new ones when the sources moved."
